**Current support.** The Alpha builder finds HTML entries from root activity. A
general `$entry` root purpose is deferred, and the earlier `$page` proposal is
superseded. Current `export:` blocks are implemented; later `$export` migration
remains deferred.

Each source directory forms at most one structural module. A normal module
uses an `@*.moth` root, while an API-only support package uses a `+*.moth` root. A
directory may contain one root of each role, forming one paired module with a
normal interface and a separately published support interface.

### One root per role

The filename after `@` or `+` does not matter. `@page.moth`, `@mod.moth` and
`@api.moth` all create the same normal-root role. One directory cannot contain
two roots of the same role. A normal root and a support root may coexist, but
they must resolve to distinct physical source files owned by that module.

### Bind the module, not the root file

Normal module-root files have no direct dependency form. Bind the directory's public module surface.

The `@` in `@page.moth` marks the file as a module root on disk. The `@` in
`@pages` starts a dependency path. They share the same symbol but are not
combined. Do not write `@@pages`. The second `@` is invalid.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    @pages
]

### Root and helper roles

The root file defines the module identity. Files without `@` remain helpers in
that module. Helpers cannot become entries or pages on their own.

A root becomes an HTML entry from authored root activity: a non-trivial root
body, a const fragment or a runtime fragment. A direct builder slot
contribution is a const fragment and still produces a complete page:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    #[$in title:Welcome]
]

A future `$entry` purpose will make entry selection explicit. It is deferred and
is not current syntax; do not write `$entry` or `$page`.

`export:` currently controls public declarations. The later `$export` spelling
will replace it; both forms won't remain permanent alternatives.
